If the bead is **closed** locally (status `closed` — **not** `deferred`, `blocked`, or any other non-terminal local status; `deferred` is local-only and must never trigger a JIRA-side write) and the JIRA ticket is **not** already in a **terminal** status (see the terminal-status allow-list), transition JIRA to a terminal status. JIRA workflows vary per project, so look up the available transitions for this ticket and pick a transition whose **target status name** matches the terminal allow-list (`Done` / `Closed` / `Resolved` / `Cancelled`/`Canceled` / `Won't Do` / `Won't Fix`/`Wontfix` / `Duplicate`, case-insensitive, exact). Prefer `Done` when multiple terminal transitions are available.

Do **not** pick a transition merely because its target `statusCategory` is `done` — deployment-pipeline statuses like `Deployed to Stage`, `Released`, or `Ready for Prod` often live in the green category but are not terminal, and transitioning there would misrepresent the ticket state.

Use **JIRA MCP tools** when available: `jira_get_transitions` to list the workflow's transitions for `<KEY>`, then `jira_transition_issue` to apply the chosen one. Otherwise use the **`jira` CLI**:

```bash
jira issue transitions <KEY>                                        # list available transitions
jira issue move <KEY> "Done" --resolution "Done" --comment "Closed in beads (<bead-id>)"
# (name of the target status may differ per workflow — fall back to whatever
# 'jira issue transitions <KEY>' lists as the done-category transition)
```

If **no** terminal transition is available (workflow restriction, permission denied, or the only reachable done-category transitions are non-terminal names like `Deployed to Stage`), do **not** force it — instead add a `[mitto]` comment saying the bead was closed locally, so a human can transition manually (via `jira_add_comment` when MCP tools are available, otherwise via `jira issue comment add <KEY> --body-file /tmp/jira-<key>.mitto.close.md`). The comment body starts with `[mitto] Closed in beads: <reason>`.

Record what was done on the bead so re-runs are idempotent:

```bash
bd update <bead-id> --set-metadata jira_pushed_status="<new-status>"
```

Skip this whole step if `metadata.jira_pushed_status` already matches the JIRA ticket's current status (nothing changed since the last push).
